            "content": [
              {
                "text": "I'm testing a rule where multi-cell clusters of 2s get surrounded by a border of 3s, while single 2s stay untouched. Checking this against the vertical pair and horizontal pair clusters, verifying the border placement accounts for clipping at grid edges.\n\nFor the two adjacent boxes near rows 12-13, each pair gets its own separate outline ring rather than a merged box, and when combined they line up correctly at row 12. So the general rule seems to be: any connected group of size two or more gets a Moore neighborhood ring of 3s drawn around it, converting zeros to threes, while isolated single 2s remain unaffected.",
                "type": "reasoning_text"
              }
